Time-Series Data in Monitoring

🔹 What is Time-Series Data? 📈
 Time-series data is data that is stored along with a timestamp.
 It helps track how values change over time.
👉 Example:
 CPU Usage → 40% at 10:00 AM
 CPU Usage → 75% at 10:05 AM
Each metric is stored with time information ⏱️

🔹 Why it matters in real-world? 🏗️
 Modern monitoring systems generate huge amounts of metrics continuously.
 Time-series data helps engineers:
✔ Analyze trends
 ✔ Detect anomalies
 ✔ Compare historical performance
 ✔ Predict future issues 🚨

🔹 Data Storage Concept: 💾
In tools like Prometheus, metrics are stored in a Time-Series Database (TSDB).
👉 Each data point contains:
Metric name
Value
Timestamp
Labels (metadata)

🔹 Example Structure: 📊
cpu_usage{server="app1"} 75 10:05AM

Here:
 ✔ cpu_usage → Metric name
 ✔ 75 → Metric value
 ✔ 10:05AM → Timestamp
 ✔ server="app1" → Label

🔹 Real-world Examples: 💡
👉 CPU usage over time
 👉 Memory consumption trends
 👉 API request count per minute
 👉 Response time history
These help teams understand system behavior 📊

🔹 Why Time-Series Data is Powerful? ⚡
✔ Helps create dashboards 📈
 ✔ Enables alerting 🚨
 ✔ Supports historical analysis
 ✔ Useful for forecasting trends

🔹 Key Concepts to Remember: 🧠
✔ Time-series = Data + Time ⏱️
 ✔ Stored in TSDB 💾
 ✔ Metrics change continuously 📊
 ✔ Helps analyze trends over time
